WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.680
Ideally, we try to center our diets around
healthy, unprocessed plant foods.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
But in today's world,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.920
most of what fills our grocery
carts isn't just processed,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.520
but ultra-processed,

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.120
which is good for shelf life,
but not necessarily our life.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.734
Let's take a closer look.

00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:24.401
"What Are Ultra-Processed Foods?"

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.320
Modern nutrition science
began about a century ago

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.000
in the context
of nutrient deficiency diseases.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.720
Editorials in the Journal of
the American Medical Association

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.400
had titles like "Sugar as Food,"
heralding sugar

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.880
as one of the cheapest sources
of calories.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.160
For six cents, you could
buy three thousand calories.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.480
But the Nutrient Deficiency era

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.360
gave way
to the Dietary Excess era.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.560
No longer were we dying
of nutrient deficiency diseases

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.200
like scurvy as much
as we were dying

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.040
from nutrient excess diseases
like obesity and heart disease.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.880
So, nutrition science
became more

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.960
about avoiding
too many calories,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.280
too much saturated fat,
too much sugar,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:05.280
and too much sodium,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.000
while still focused
on nutrients.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.840
This allowed food companies
to get away with whipping out

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.480
fiber-fortified Froot Loops.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.280
But "Food, Not Nutrients, is the
Fundamental Unit of Nutrition."

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.360
And to its credit,
the field of nutrition

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.800
started moving
towards a more holistic view.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.800
First generation
dietary guidelines

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.720
emphasized individual nutrients,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.360
then moved to second generation
food-based dietary guidelines,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.840
which largely converged
on encouraging diets

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:37.840
rich in vegetables,
fruits, legumes

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.680
(beans, split peas,
chickpeas, and lentils),

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.920
whole grains, and nuts.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.080
But an area
of emerging importance

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.520
is the degree
of food processing.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.840
National nutrition guidelines
first told us

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.840
to cut down on saturated fat,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.000
alcohol, cholesterol,
salt, and sugar.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.080
Then they started actually
naming names,

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.120
suggesting citizens might want
to cut down on cake,

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.240
for example.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.960
So, from nutrients to foods,
and more recently,

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.520
countries recommending
limiting processed foods.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.120
This was the bombshell

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.880
that really started
the momentum,

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.480
a commentary in the journal
Public Health Nutrition in 2009

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.840
by Carlos Monteiro,
a nutrition professor

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.880
at the University of Sao Paulo.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.200
Maybe the issue
with nutrition and health

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.960
is not so much the food,
nor the nutrients,

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.960
as much as the level
of processing.

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.520
For example,
food-based dietary guidelines

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.480
might tell us
to eat more vegetable soup.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.680
Great! But there’s
vegetable soup,

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.920
then there’s vegetable soup.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.440
Are we talking about

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.240
cleaning-out-your-fridge
vegetable soup?

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.240
A health-haloed organic quinoa
and kale soup

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.480
with a heart-stopping

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.680
1,200 milligrams of sodium
per can?

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.280
How about a vegetable soup
that has more salt

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.200
than it has vegetables?

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.760
Or a vegetable flavored soup

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.800
that contains
more artificial flavors and MSG

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.720
than it has vegetables.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.200
Just like there
is breakfast cereal

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.320
and then there
is breakfast cereal.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.560
Professor Monteiro
and colleagues

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.760
proposed the "NOVA"
food classification system.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.440
NOVA is not an acronym.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.440
It means new in Portuguese,

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.760
as in a new way
to classify foods,

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:27.760
based on the level
of processing.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.160
NOVA classifies all foods
and food products

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:31.200
into four groups.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.720
Group 1 foods are unprocessed
or minimally processed,

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.440
like fresh, dried,
frozen, or cooked plants

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.680
or animal parts, with nothing
bad added, like salt or sugar.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.440
Group 2 foods are the salt,
sugar, and fats used in cooking,

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.720
and Group 3 includes
traditionally processed foods,

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.800
like when you add
Group 2 ingredients

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.280
to Group 1 foods.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.560
But what really put NOVA
on the map is Group 4,

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.720
its concept
of ultra-processed foods.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.320
These are
industrial formulations

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:03.840
of several ingredients which,

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.680
besides salt, sugar,
oils and fats,

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.440
include food substances not used
in culinary preparations,

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.400
like added flavors, colors,
artificial sweeteners,

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.840
emulsifiers, and other additives
used to imitate real foods.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.720
Like a frosted grape pop-tart

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.200
that has more grapes on
the front of the box

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.280
than inside it,
with less grapes than salt,

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.080
but may artificially
taste like grapes

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.800
and look like grapes
because of one,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.880
two, three, four,
five different food dyes.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.280
"Simply put, ultra-processed
foods are foods

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:39.280
that can’t be made
in home kitchens

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.280
because they have
been chemically

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.400
or physically transformed
using industrial processes."

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.600
"They typically contain
little or no whole foods,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.360
are ready-to-consume or heat up,

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.280
and are fatty, salty,
or sugary

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:53.040
and depleted in dietary fiber,"

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.600
and other nutrients-
like all the sweet,

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.400
fatty, or salty snacks
like potato chips, ice cream,

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.960
soda, candy, French fries,
burgers, hot dogs,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.360
chicken nuggets,
fish sticks.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.920
Basically, almost everything
in a box or a bag.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.040
Why not just call them,
"Packaged foods"?

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.480
Well, they were
thinking about it,

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.440
but were afraid consumers
might look at a bag of apples

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.320
or something and get confused.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.360
Before the NOVA classification
system was introduced,

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.320
the issue of food processing
was largely ignored,

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.480
or minimized,
in education and outreach,

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.960
as well as in public
health policies.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:30.000
But NOVA exploded
onto the stage,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.320
resulting in a super-NOVA
in the world of nutrition.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.760
But what exactly
is so revolutionary

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.080
about this concept
of ultra-processed foods?

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.640
I mean, wasn’t fatty, salty,
sugary junk always a bad idea?

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.440
What’s so new about NOVA?

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.200
Monteiro suggests
that there are harmful effects

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.240
of ultra-processed foods
that are independent

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.840
of their nutrient profile.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.440
Is that true?

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.160
That’s exactly what
I’ll cover next.

